<title>GERMANY: Tietmeyer says EMU delay rumours nonsense.</title>
<headline>Tietmeyer says EMU delay rumours nonsense.</headline>
<dateline>FRANKFURT 1997-03-03</dateline>
<text>
<p>German central bank president Hans Tietmeyer said on Monday recent speculation that European economic and monetary union (EMU) could be delayed was nonsense.</p>
<p>&quot;All rumours referring to EMU delay are not in line with reality,&quot; Tietmeyer told reporters at a banking conference in Frankfurt.</p>
<p>Tietmeyer, affirming that he believed Germany still had a good chance of meeting criteria for monetary union entry despite unemployment problems, said he saw new momentum in the German economy shortly.</p>
<p>&quot;The growth pause of the last quarter of last year will be over in a short time,&quot; he said. &quot;I expect new growth developments by the spring.&quot;</p>
<p>But he said that while he was concerned by the current high level of unemployment, no &quot;patent recipe&quot; for a cure exists.</p>
<p>--Frankfurt Newsroom +49 69 755625</p>
</text>
